 xmlns:trans="http://docbook.org/ns/transclusion">
  <info>
    <title>Viewing and parsing <filename>/var/log</filename> files</title>
    <!-- can be changed via merge
      in the assembly -->
    <!-- add author's e-mail -->
    <meta name="maintainer" content="souvik.sarkar@suse.com" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract>
      <!-- can be changed via merge in the assembly -->
      <para>
        Plain-text logs in <filename>/var/log</filename> can be viewed,
        searched, and parsed with standard command-line tools.
      </para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <para>
    For viewing log files, use the commands <command>less</command> or
    <command>more</command>. Use <command>head</command> and
    <command>tail</command> to view the beginning or end of a log file. To view
    entries appended to a log file in real time, use <command>tail</command>
    <option>-f</option>. For information about how to use these tools, see
    their man pages.
  </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;tail -f /var/log/messages</screen>
  <para>
    To search for strings or regular expressions in log files, use
    <command>grep</command>. <command>awk</command> is useful for parsing and
    rewriting log files. For example, to show only lines logged by the
    <literal>sshd-session</literal> process:
  </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;grep sshd-session /var/log/messages</screen>
  <para>
    Rotated log files are compressed (see
    <xref linkend="logging-logrotate-configuration"/>). &productnameshort;
    compresses them with <command>xz</command> by default, so use
    <command>xzless</command>, <command>xzcat</command>, or
    <command>xzgrep</command> instead of the <command>less</command>,
    <command>cat</command>, or <command>grep</command> commands above:
  </para>
<screen>&prompt.sudo;xzgrep sshd-session /var/log/messages-20260828.xz</screen>
</topic>
